\chapter{Architecture}

The system has these parts. Each one becomes a concept chapter later.

\begin{quote}
\texttt{React UI (Tailwind)} \,---\, lessons, modes, panels, quiz feedback. It
updates on discrete events only, never on each frame.

\texttt{Rope model (Rapier)} \,---\, rigid links joined by joints.

\texttt{Renderer (three.js)} \,---\, one continuous tube, anchors and the frame.
It is owned by one component.

\texttt{Snapshot} \,---\, a read-only copy of positions, passed to the topology.

\texttt{Topology} \,---\, closes the rope, projects it, builds a crossing code,
and computes invariants.

\texttt{Recogniser} \,---\, matches the invariants against the knot library.

\texttt{Lessons} \,---\, data records that the three modes read.
\end{quote}

\section{Decision: rendering in three.js}

We use three.js. The brief asks for it, and it runs in a browser with no
install. The alternatives are a game engine such as Unity or Godot, which is
heavier and not browser-first, and a 2D canvas, which cannot show the depth
that the topology needs.

\section{Decision: the rope is a Rapier chain, drawn as one tube}

The rope is a chain of short rigid links joined neighbour to neighbour, using
Rapier. The engine version is pinned at scaffold time.

The length of the rope is kept by a rope joint. This is a maximum-distance
constraint, so it allows a little slack. Minimal tension is a weak pull toward
a straight line.

The rope cannot pass through itself or through an anchor, because Rapier's
contacts keep the links apart. Continuous collision detection is switched on
for the links, so that a fast drag does not pass a link through another.

Each joint is created with contacts between its two links disabled, so
neighbouring links never push on each other. Collision groups keep the links
from touching other links where they should not.

The grab point is a kinematic body moved by the mouse. A kinematic body is not
pushed by contacts, so the rope never blocks the player's hand.

Each frame, the link centres are smoothed with a Catmull-Rom curve. The tube's
vertices are rewritten into a buffer that is allocated once. The rope
therefore never looks like a set of links.

The alternatives considered were these. A custom position-based solver would
need segment-to-segment collision written from scratch, which is the hardest
part of the rope; Rapier already provides it, and its types confirm the joints
and collision controls used here. A Rapier soft-body rope, built with
\texttt{SoftBodyDesc.rope}, has self-contact options; it is the fallback if the
chain proves unstable in stage one. Lines built with \texttt{Line2} or
\texttt{MeshLine} are thin, and forum reports describe memory growth when they
are updated each frame. Rebuilding \texttt{TubeGeometry} each frame costs an
allocation each time.

\paragraph{Gate result.} The spike measured the chain before the rest of stage
one was built. With no limit, the tail of a forty-link rope dragged at 1.5 m/s
reached 8 to 15 m/s, and the soft-body rope did the same. Both were rejected as
unstable. The chain is kept with three settings: 32 solver iterations, linear
damping of 0.5, and a link speed cap of 3 m/s applied after each physics step.
With these, the stretch stays within 1.03 of the rope length, the figure-eight
produces no overlap between links, no link enters the post, and a forty-link
step costs about 0.7 ms. The cap is a deliberate departure from pure physics:
at a hand speed of 1.5 m/s the tail sits at the cap, so the rope moves more
slowly than the physics alone would move it. The soft-body rope is not used.

Rendering runs on demand. The frame loop runs while the rope moves and stops
when it settles, as the three.js manual advises for scenes that change only on
input.

\section{Decision: closure is a point behind the background}

For recognition only, the closure described in the analysis is used. The join
lies behind everything, so the crossings it makes count as passing under the
rope. This needs no special case in the topology code.

\section{Decision: recognition by planar projection and invariants}

The closed curve is projected onto the view plane. Each crossing and its
over-or-under is read from depth, and the crossings are written as a Planar
Diagram code. From that code two invariants are computed: the determinant,
counted by Fox colouring, and the Alexander polynomial, from the Wirtinger
presentation. A knot is matched against a library only when both invariants
agree.

The recogniser reads a snapshot of the link positions. It never changes the
rope or the physics world. It runs off the frame loop, throttled, and can move
to a Web Worker if profiling shows it blocks frames.

The alternatives were these. Matching the pose against stored templates fails
invariant 2, because the pose changes with every drag. The determinant alone
cannot separate some knots.

The invariants do not separate every pair of knots, and they cannot tell a
mirror image from its original. The library is therefore checked for collisions
at build time, and the taught set is checked before lesson content is written.

\section{Decision: anchors are closed components}

Each anchor is a closed loop in space. A carabiner, a bar, a ring and a tree
trunk are all loops. Anchors are separate components, so a hitch around an
anchor is a link between the rope and the anchor. The recogniser handles
several components in the same way as one.

Modelling an anchor as a fixed point cannot represent a rope wrapped around
it. Modelling the anchor inside the rope merges two topologies, which is wrong.

\section{Decision: lessons are data}

Each knot is one data record. It holds the name, the stage, the web sources,
the invariants, the guided steps and the quiz prompt. Guided, sandbox and quiz
modes all read the same records. Quiz evaluation compares the recognised knot
with the prompt. A hard-coded lesson would turn every change into a code
change, and a remote content service is not needed for a proof of concept.

\section{Decision: literate source with weft}

The source of truth is these \texttt{.weft} chapters. The \texttt{weft} tool
tangles them into \texttt{src/}. We built weft 2.0.2 from its repository and
copied the binary to the user's local bin directory.

\section{Decision: tooling}

Vite is the dev server and build tool. React is the UI. Tailwind styles the UI
only, never the 3D canvas. Vitest runs the tests. three.js and Rapier are
pinned to exact versions.

The 3D scene is owned by one component. That component creates the renderer,
the scene and the physics world in an effect, and disposes of them on unmount.

The research behind the rendering choices comes from the three.js manual,
which is in the three.js source repository. Its advice is these. Merging
geometry reduces draw calls. Geometries, materials and textures are not freed
automatically, so \texttt{dispose()} must be called when they are no longer
used. A continuously animated loop is wasteful for a scene that changes only on
input.

\section{Forbidden edges}

These are rules that a change must not break. They are checked in review.

\begin{enumerate}
\item The rope model does not import three.js.
\item The renderer never writes rope state.
\item The recogniser reads a snapshot and never changes the rope, the physics
world or the scene.
\item Topology does not import React or three.js.
\item React holds no per-frame state. Per-frame data is written to refs.
\item Lesson records are data, with no code and no three.js or Rapier objects.
\item Anchors are scene data. The rope model does not own anchor geometry.
\item One component creates and disposes of the physics world and the renderer.
No other code does.
\item Tailwind does not style the canvas.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Named seams}

These contracts are empty until stage one.

\begin{description}
\item[RopeSnapshot] The link centres, the radius and the two ends. Read-only.
\item[AnchorSnapshot] A closed loop of points, one per anchor.
\item[KnotResult] A name, or nothing if the knot is not recognised, together
with its invariants.
\item[LessonRecord] The data described above.
\end{description}
